11. Patents.

A "contributor" is a copyright holder who authorizes use of the Program or a work on which it is based.  The licensed work is the contributor's "contributor version".

A contributor's "essential patent claims" are those controlled claims infringed by permitted making, use, or sale of the contributor version, excluding claims infringed only by further modification.  "Control" includes authority to grant sublicenses consistent with this License.

Each contributor grants a non-exclusive, worldwide, royalty-free patent license under essential claims to make, use, sell, offer, import, run, modify, and propagate the contributor version.

A "patent license" is any agreement not to enforce a patent.  To "grant" one means agreeing not to enforce it against a party.

If you convey a covered work knowingly relying on a patent license and corresponding source is not freely available through a public server or accessible means, you must make it available, lose the benefit, or extend the patent license downstream.  "Knowingly relying" means actual knowledge that otherwise the conveyance or use would infringe identifiable patents believed valid.

If in one arrangement you convey or procure conveyance and grant a patent license to some recipients, it extends automatically to all recipients and works based on the covered work.

A patent license is "discriminatory" if it excludes, prohibits, or conditions exercise of rights specifically granted.  You may not convey under certain payment arrangements with software distributors that grant discriminatory patent licenses, unless entered before 28 March 2007.

Nothing excludes or limits implied licenses or other defenses under patent law.

